{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x+y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y(-x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{3x}({x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3y}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}xyz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}xy(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-2{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2})\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\sqrt{2}({x}^{2}y-x{y}^{2})"] ["math" "-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{2}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{2})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5x}(2{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4x+4y}"] ["math" "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "({x}^{4}{y}^{4}+{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(4y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(4{x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}x)\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "(6{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "({x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}{x}^{3}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{x}^{2}y(y-2x)^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}\\,x{y}^{2}(y+2x)^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}\\,{x}^{2}y(y-2x)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ and $-x\\le y\\le x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161355" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161355" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161355" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+8y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+8y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+8y)=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4x+8y}{36x}\\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,9\\frac{4(x+2y)}{36x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(1+\\frac{2y}{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[y+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x}-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le e.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{(2x+y)-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{y=1}^{y=e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}"] ["math" "e+3"] ["math" "e-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=5x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x-y)=5,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+(5x-y)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\sqrt{27}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[26{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-5x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(52{x}^{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-20{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{104{x}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}104{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[26{x}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =26\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25+\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "25\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "26\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "5+\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{10}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "26\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-2\\le y\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(2xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{2}]_{y=-2}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/5d6cedcc2d8c5a232d8d7ed89f050fa7a545ed0da6075ddb44eec67e065b9e87.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=-4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/9215246969b621002a96dec3aed8a012045b8061371e4c6a1234f54d53de29cc.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{3}y$ whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(e-1)\\sqrt{2}\\pi "] ["math" "({e}^{4}-1)\\pi "] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\pi "] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi "] ["math" "{e}^{4}\\sqrt{5}\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/d19d54d0e76ced24d1bd9f865578b72f2b8d67414c8506499c6ae7b1300a6275.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-2x+2y$ and whose domain is the left half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/0148ca16ee12a0f651c91091715b4ec07d95639530defb8ed366bacb33e4e87d.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}(1+1) \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16\\sqrt{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/5276afad13c3e52854d33a3ff3404ab401781e08d1888214121cf2d56c0bb304.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+2y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32(1+1) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aff47a430aa5f8363d304fc120d517b7aa81d5c9494060f7a170c5a9ee3fb2de"} {"question" "q-161301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "48"] ["math" "56"] ["math" "60"] ["math" "64"] ["math" "72"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "q-161301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "64"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/state.json" "file_sha256" "a18c45eafebf682fe8c1540b9f0e37cedd1dbaacaeadfad798ef110041217271"} {"question" "e-12382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/e44b57bad88dcc1c13680f0e8f110696c6975b16c1d9553138fb6aeb4cadb8f9.png)\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/example-12382.json" "file_sha256" "3c5a4065ff6b915262e4a96c5017357d607237b200574f8b2c31fdaf465a5e3b"} {"question" "e-12382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/example-12382.json" "file_sha256" "3c5a4065ff6b915262e4a96c5017357d607237b200574f8b2c31fdaf465a5e3b"} {"question" "e-12380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^2}} \\: \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/example-12380.json" "file_sha256" "0d29f565b6b4ed3027139a804211983ab92ff0d6605b7c00d625b59e895b5f68"} {"question" "e-12380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+x+{y}^{2})-x+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2(1+2{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/example-12380.json" "file_sha256" "0d29f565b6b4ed3027139a804211983ab92ff0d6605b7c00d625b59e895b5f68"} {"question" "e-12381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/d8675cb0c59ba915cefe4b0bbe539f1e7e38b5a3ac2fae4bd2bb25e4de96e116.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/example-12381.json" "file_sha256" "8dcaa06838758561cdbb9e8bb5da58fd25b3d5361aaa8c173e79f60e3b579e5b"} {"question" "e-12381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/example-12381.json" "file_sha256" "8dcaa06838758561cdbb9e8bb5da58fd25b3d5361aaa8c173e79f60e3b579e5b"}] "usage" {"e-12381" [] "e-12382" [] "e-12380" [] "q-161355" []} "basis" 2209 "retractions" [[17592186105989 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$∬_(S)(z)/(\\sqrt{1 + 2\\cos^{2} (x + y)})dS$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = \\sin (x + y)$ for $0 \\le x \\le π$ and $-x \\le y \\le x$."] [17592186105382 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz$, where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$.\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below.\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186105382 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (2x)^{2} + (-1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x(x^{2} - y)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - xy)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}$."] [17592186105380 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n$\\iint\\limits_{S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^2}} \\: \\textrm{d}S$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1$."] [17592186105380 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z - x + y^{2}}}dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(1)/(\\sqrt{(1 + x + y^{2}) - x + y^{2}}) \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 1^{2} + (2y)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2 + 4y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2(1 + 2y^{2})}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{2}dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (1 - 0) \\cdot (1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105381 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S$,\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1264/Source/Images/8fda75933a423952bc1fd00fcda06419.png)"] [17592186105381 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)x^{2}ydS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{2}y \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} + (-1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{2}y \\cdot \\sqrt{4}dA \\\\\n&= 2∬_(D)x^{2}ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∬_(D)x^{2}ydA &= 2∬_(Δ)f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∬_(Δ)r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ \\cdot r\\sin θ \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∬_(Δ)r^{4}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θ∫_{0}^{1}r^{4}drdθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θ[\\frac{r^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{15}[- \\cos^{3} θ]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{15}(1 + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186102182 17592186102183 17592186102184 17592186102185 17592186102186] "immutable_fields" [[17592186102181 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102181 :answer-field/id] [17592186102181 :answer-field/key] [17592186102181 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
